--TEST--
`Core\Process::run` asks `process.exec` first and the target's kind second, so the same `.bat` file is a capability refusal outside the grant and a target-kind refusal inside it — `rule:core-classes/process-refuses-a-shell-target` and `rule:security/process-exec-capability`, `rule:security/capability-declaration-is-one-table`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Two identical files, one inside the granted root and one outside it. The
// only difference between the two calls below is the path, so whichever
// refusal each gets is the one the door reached first — and a member that
// looked at the extension before the grant would answer "will not run" to
// both, disclosing what kind of thing sits at a path this program was never
// allowed to ask about.
try {
    var $outside = Core\Process::run("other/say.bat", []);
    echo "outside: accepted, exit=", $outside->exitCode(),
         " err=", Core\Bytes::length($outside->stderr()), "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $denied) {
    if (Core\Str::contains($denied->message, "needs the capability `process.exec` for ")) {
        echo "outside: the capability answered first\n";
    } else {
        echo "outside: refused by target kind\n";
    }
}

// Inside the grant, and now the kind is what decides. The refusal names the
// extension rather than the file, because it is about which program the
// operating system would hand the command line to.
try {
    var $inside = Core\Process::run("allowed/say.bat", []);
    echo "inside: accepted, exit=", $inside->exitCode(),
         " out=", Core\Bytes::length($inside->stdout()), "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    if (Core\Str::contains($refused->message, "a `.bat` target is started by handing a command line")) {
        echo "inside: refused by target kind\n";
    } else {
        echo "inside: the capability answered first\n";
    }
}

// The third answer, which is what makes the first two mean anything: a granted
// target of a kind this API will start reaches the operating system, and the
// operating system is what says no. `IOError` is caught before `RuntimeError`
// so the case names which half of the door ran.
try {
    var $missing = Core\Process::run("allowed/absent", []);
    echo "absent: accepted, exit=", $missing->exitCode(),
         " err=", Core\Bytes::length($missing->stderr()), "\n";
} catch (IOError $os) {
    echo "absent: the os answered\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $stopped) {
    echo "absent: stopped before the os\n";
}
--FILE allowed/say.bat--
@echo off
echo hello
--FILE other/say.bat--
@echo off
echo hello
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.process]
exec = ["allowed"]
--EXPECT--
outside: the capability answered first
inside: refused by target kind
absent: the os answered
